1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,325
In the autumn of 1950 the war

2
00:00:02,345 --> 00:00:04,407
in Korea seemed almost over.

3
00:00:04,496 --> 00:00:06,298
American and South Korean troops

4
00:00:06,318 --> 00:00:08,096
had smashed the North Korean

5
00:00:08,116 --> 00:00:10,508
army after the landing at Inchon.

6
00:00:10,912 --> 00:00:12,852
They had taken Pyongyang.

7
00:00:13,007 --> 00:00:14,945
Now they were racing north toward the

8
00:00:14,965 --> 00:00:17,719
Yalu River and the border with China.

9
00:00:18,153 --> 00:00:20,512
General Douglas MacArthur was confident.

10
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:22,421
Word spread through the ranks that

11
00:00:22,441 --> 00:00:24,834
the boys would be home by Christmas.

12
00:00:24,961 --> 00:00:27,411
On November 24 he launched what he believed

13
00:00:27,431 --> 00:00:29,936
would be the final offensive of the war.

14
00:00:30,341 --> 00:00:32,000
On the eastern side of the

15
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:34,430
peninsula the job fell to X Corps.

16
00:00:34,486 --> 00:00:35,798
At its spearhead was the

17
00:00:35,818 --> 00:00:37,755
First Marine Division.

18
00:00:37,853 --> 00:00:39,380
15,000 men under Major

19
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:41,589
General Oliver Prince Smith.

20
00:00:41,983 --> 00:00:43,853
Their road led north from the port

21
00:00:43,873 --> 00:00:46,210
of Hungnam into the high country

22
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:48,771
around a man-made lake the Americans

23
00:00:48,791 --> 00:00:51,104
called the Chosin Reservoir.

24
00:00:51,559 --> 00:00:53,655
Smith did not like what he saw.

25
00:00:53,689 --> 00:00:55,148
His division was being strung

26
00:00:55,168 --> 00:00:57,665
out along a single narrow road.

27
00:00:58,116 --> 00:00:59,980
78 miles of dirt and gravel

28
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,631
climbed from the coast into mountains

29
00:01:02,651 --> 00:01:04,685
more than 4,000 feet high.

30
00:01:04,830 --> 00:01:05,993
One road in.

31
00:01:06,116 --> 00:01:07,314
One road out.

32
00:01:07,369 --> 00:01:08,270
The planners called it

33
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:09,761
the Main Supply Route.

34
00:01:09,815 --> 00:01:12,657
The Marines simply called it the MSR.

35
00:01:13,043 --> 00:01:14,729
His corps commander Major General

36
00:01:14,749 --> 00:01:17,289
Edward Almond wanted speed.

37
00:01:17,379 --> 00:01:18,941
Smith wanted caution.

38
00:01:19,109 --> 00:01:21,456
So he quietly slowed his advance.

39
00:01:21,872 --> 00:01:23,730
He stockpiled ammunition at a

40
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:26,529
mountain village called Hagaru-ri.

41
00:01:26,627 --> 00:01:28,497
He ordered his engineers to begin cutting

42
00:01:28,517 --> 00:01:30,989
an airstrip into the frozen ground.

43
00:01:31,157 --> 00:01:33,458
Some officers thought he was being timid.

44
00:01:33,578 --> 00:01:35,004
Within days that caution

45
00:01:35,024 --> 00:01:36,722
would save his division.

46
00:01:37,261 --> 00:01:39,166
Because the mountains were not empty.

47
00:01:39,616 --> 00:01:42,396
Since October an entire Chinese army

48
00:01:42,416 --> 00:01:44,854
group had been slipping across the Yalu.

49
00:01:44,931 --> 00:01:46,732
The Ninth Army Group under

50
00:01:46,752 --> 00:01:48,404
General Song Shilun.

51
00:01:48,552 --> 00:01:49,954
12 divisions.

52
00:01:50,083 --> 00:01:52,374
Roughly 120,000 men.

53
00:01:52,759 --> 00:01:54,166
They marched at night.

54
00:01:54,261 --> 00:01:57,006
They hid in villages and forests by day.

55
00:01:57,026 --> 00:01:58,576
They lit no fires.

56
00:01:58,665 --> 00:02:00,285
American reconnaissance planes

57
00:02:00,305 --> 00:02:02,072
flew over the same ridges again

58
00:02:02,092 --> 00:02:04,389
and again and saw nothing.

59
00:02:04,783 --> 00:02:06,269
There had been warnings.

60
00:02:06,359 --> 00:02:08,194
In early November the Seventh Marines

61
00:02:08,214 --> 00:02:10,004
fought a sharp battle near a village

62
00:02:10,024 --> 00:02:12,757
called Sudong against Chinese troops.

63
00:02:12,926 --> 00:02:14,487
Prisoners were taken.

64
00:02:14,542 --> 00:02:16,404
They said many more were coming.

65
00:02:16,857 --> 00:02:19,261
But in Tokyo MacArthur's headquarters did

66
00:02:19,281 --> 00:02:22,152
not believe China would intervene in force.

67
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,129
Mao Zedong had a different plan.

68
00:02:25,252 --> 00:02:27,530
He did not want to push the Americans back.

69
00:02:27,585 --> 00:02:29,185
He wanted to destroy them.

70
00:02:29,593 --> 00:02:30,734
Let the Marines climb

71
00:02:30,754 --> 00:02:32,089
deep into the mountains.

72
00:02:32,109 --> 00:02:33,466
Let them stretch their line

73
00:02:33,486 --> 00:02:35,650
thin along that single road.

74
00:02:35,757 --> 00:02:37,421
Then cut it into pieces and

75
00:02:37,441 --> 00:02:40,085
annihilate each piece one by one.

76
00:02:40,486 --> 00:02:41,429
By the last week of

77
00:02:41,449 --> 00:02:43,926
November the trap was set.

78
00:02:43,985 --> 00:02:45,763
The Fifth and Seventh Marines had

79
00:02:45,783 --> 00:02:47,664
pushed west of the reservoir to a

80
00:02:47,684 --> 00:02:50,214
cluster of huts called Yudam-ni.

81
00:02:50,263 --> 00:02:52,269
Behind them small garrisons

82
00:02:52,289 --> 00:02:54,487
held Hagaru-ri and Koto-ri.

83
00:02:54,573 --> 00:02:56,876
East of the reservoir an Army regimental

84
00:02:56,896 --> 00:02:59,469
combat team was moving into position.

85
00:02:59,852 --> 00:03:02,572
And all around them in the snow lay

86
00:03:02,592 --> 00:03:05,559
120,000 men waiting for the signal.

87
00:03:05,605 --> 00:03:07,516
Then the temperature began to fall.

88
00:03:07,915 --> 00:03:09,191
The cold arrived like an

89
00:03:09,211 --> 00:03:11,011
enemy offensive of its own.

90
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:12,935
A Siberian front swept down

91
00:03:12,955 --> 00:03:14,608
over the high plateau.

92
00:03:14,777 --> 00:03:16,595
In a single day the temperature

93
00:03:16,615 --> 00:03:18,722
dropped by more than 40 degrees.

94
00:03:19,152 --> 00:03:21,840
At night it fell to 20 below zero.

95
00:03:21,963 --> 00:03:23,388
Then 30 below.

96
00:03:23,484 --> 00:03:26,719
On the worst nights it approached 40 below.

97
00:03:26,817 --> 00:03:28,208
The wind howling through the

98
00:03:28,228 --> 00:03:30,711
passes made it feel even colder.

99
00:03:31,174 --> 00:03:32,974
Nothing worked the way it should.

100
00:03:33,143 --> 00:03:35,376
Gun oil turned thick as glue.

101
00:03:35,396 --> 00:03:38,051
Carbines jammed after a single shot.

102
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:39,668
Browning Automatic Rifles

103
00:03:39,688 --> 00:03:42,205
fired slowly or not at all.

104
00:03:42,372 --> 00:03:43,957
Mortar baseplates cracked when

105
00:03:43,977 --> 00:03:45,812
they struck the frozen earth.

106
00:03:45,922 --> 00:03:47,803
Artillery shells fell short because

107
00:03:47,823 --> 00:03:50,351
the powder burned slowly in the cold.

108
00:03:51,121 --> 00:03:52,535
Batteries died.

109
00:03:52,589 --> 00:03:54,846
Truck engines had to be run all night

110
00:03:54,866 --> 00:03:56,780
or they would never start again.

111
00:03:56,896 --> 00:04:00,051
Blood plasma froze solid in its bottles.

112
00:04:00,174 --> 00:04:02,158
Medics carried morphine syrettes in

113
00:04:02,178 --> 00:04:04,926
their mouths to keep them from freezing.

114
00:04:05,338 --> 00:04:08,505
Canned rations froze into blocks of ice.

115
00:04:08,629 --> 00:04:11,488
A Marine who warmed a can over a small fire

116
00:04:11,508 --> 00:04:14,651
would eat hot food around a frozen core.

117
00:04:14,797 --> 00:04:17,153
Dysentery spread through the ranks.

118
00:04:17,590 --> 00:04:19,891
Frostbite was everywhere.

119
00:04:20,059 --> 00:04:22,088
Feet sweated inside rubber boots called

120
00:04:22,108 --> 00:04:25,206
shoe-pacs during the day and froze at night.

121
00:04:25,375 --> 00:04:27,039
Fingers lost all feeling

122
00:04:27,059 --> 00:04:29,121
inside thick mittens.

123
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:31,200
Men who pulled off a glove to fire

124
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:32,965
a rifle could lose the skin of

125
00:04:32,985 --> 00:04:35,149
their hand to the frozen steel.

126
00:04:35,719 --> 00:04:38,407
The ground was frozen nearly a foot deep.

127
00:04:38,576 --> 00:04:40,114
Shovels bounced off it.

128
00:04:40,238 --> 00:04:41,742
Men used explosives just

129
00:04:41,762 --> 00:04:43,484
to blast out a foxhole.

130
00:04:43,564 --> 00:04:45,707
Others built walls of rock and packed

131
00:04:45,727 --> 00:04:48,470
snow and lay behind them all night.

132
00:04:48,855 --> 00:04:51,247
The terrain made everything worse.

133
00:04:51,316 --> 00:04:52,935
The reservoir sat on a high

134
00:04:52,955 --> 00:04:55,405
plateau ringed by peaks.

135
00:04:55,494 --> 00:04:57,101
Ridges climbed steeply from

136
00:04:57,121 --> 00:04:58,990
the road on both sides.

137
00:04:59,049 --> 00:05:00,429
Whoever held the high ground

138
00:05:00,449 --> 00:05:02,114
could fire straight down onto

139
00:05:02,134 --> 00:05:04,808
anything that moved along the MSR.

140
00:05:05,197 --> 00:05:06,717
And the road itself was barely

141
00:05:06,737 --> 00:05:08,707
wide enough for a single truck.

142
00:05:08,778 --> 00:05:10,500
In places it clung to cliffs

143
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,906
with a sheer drop on one side.

144
00:05:13,301 --> 00:05:15,613
Yet the cold did not choose sides.

145
00:05:15,701 --> 00:05:18,017
The Chinese suffered even more.

146
00:05:18,061 --> 00:05:19,259
Many wore quilted cotton

147
00:05:19,279 --> 00:05:21,456
uniforms and canvas shoes.

148
00:05:21,476 --> 00:05:23,144
Some had no gloves at all.

149
00:05:23,553 --> 00:05:25,021
They had marched for weeks on

150
00:05:25,041 --> 00:05:27,821
handfuls of rice and frozen potatoes.

151
00:05:27,921 --> 00:05:29,790
Whole units lost more men to

152
00:05:29,810 --> 00:05:32,259
frostbite than to American bullets.

153
00:05:32,428 --> 00:05:34,514
Later some Chinese soldiers would be found

154
00:05:34,534 --> 00:05:36,881
frozen to death in their fighting positions

155
00:05:36,901 --> 00:05:39,487
with their weapons still in their hands.

156
00:05:39,898 --> 00:05:41,926
This was the stage for one of the most

157
00:05:41,946 --> 00:05:44,624
brutal battles of the twentieth century.

158
00:05:44,707 --> 00:05:47,032
Two armies would fight on a frozen plateau

159
00:05:47,052 --> 00:05:49,194
where the weather killed without mercy.

160
00:05:49,235 --> 00:05:51,354
And on the night of November

161
00:05:51,374 --> 00:05:53,815
27 the bugles began to sound.

162
00:05:54,204 --> 00:05:55,715
It started in the dark.

163
00:05:55,770 --> 00:05:56,591
Whistles.

164
00:05:56,612 --> 00:05:57,525
Bugles.

165
00:05:57,545 --> 00:05:58,880
Shepherd's horns.

166
00:05:59,048 --> 00:06:01,864
Then shapes moving across the snow.

167
00:06:02,261 --> 00:06:04,847
At Yudam-ni the Fifth and Seventh Marines

168
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:06,873
had settled in around the village.

169
00:06:06,971 --> 00:06:08,146
They held the surrounding

170
00:06:08,166 --> 00:06:09,910
ridges in a rough ring.

171
00:06:10,026 --> 00:06:11,969
That night 2 Chinese divisions

172
00:06:11,989 --> 00:06:14,063
came for them and a third moved

173
00:06:14,083 --> 00:06:16,131
to cut the road behind them.

174
00:06:16,528 --> 00:06:18,551
The attackers came in waves.

175
00:06:18,622 --> 00:06:20,069
They hit the hills the Marines

176
00:06:20,089 --> 00:06:22,073
knew by their map numbers.

177
00:06:22,173 --> 00:06:23,802
Hill 1282.

178
00:06:23,823 --> 00:06:25,135
Hill 1240.

179
00:06:25,155 --> 00:06:26,747
Hill 1403.

180
00:06:27,154 --> 00:06:29,547
Grenades bounced down the slopes.

181
00:06:29,568 --> 00:06:31,164
Machine guns fired until

182
00:06:31,184 --> 00:06:32,734
their barrels glowed.

183
00:06:32,755 --> 00:06:34,977
Marines fought from foxholes they had

184
00:06:34,997 --> 00:06:37,446
barely scraped into the frozen ground.

185
00:06:38,225 --> 00:06:40,880
Company after company was cut to pieces.

186
00:06:40,968 --> 00:06:42,416
Some positions held.

187
00:06:42,505 --> 00:06:43,873
Some were overrun and

188
00:06:43,893 --> 00:06:45,971
then retaken before dawn.

189
00:06:45,999 --> 00:06:48,659
On Hill 1282 one Marine company

190
00:06:48,679 --> 00:06:51,681
was reduced to a handful of men.

191
00:06:51,806 --> 00:06:54,302
Cooks and clerks and drivers grabbed rifles

192
00:06:54,322 --> 00:06:56,765
and climbed the hill to fill the gaps.

193
00:06:57,145 --> 00:06:59,385
By morning the snow around Yudam-ni

194
00:06:59,405 --> 00:07:01,764
was dark with fallen soldiers.

195
00:07:01,803 --> 00:07:03,479
The Marines had held.

196
00:07:03,559 --> 00:07:04,678
But they soon learned the

197
00:07:04,698 --> 00:07:06,422
true scale of the attack.

198
00:07:06,805 --> 00:07:09,638
The Chinese had not only struck at Yudam-ni.

199
00:07:09,658 --> 00:07:12,050
They had struck everywhere at once.

200
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:15,072
The road south to Hagaru-ri was cut.

201
00:07:15,139 --> 00:07:17,794
At Toktong Pass halfway down a single rifle

202
00:07:17,814 --> 00:07:20,593
company was surrounded on a lonely hill.

203
00:07:20,716 --> 00:07:23,736
At Hagaru-ri itself enemy troops stormed

204
00:07:23,756 --> 00:07:25,829
toward the half-finished airstrip.

205
00:07:25,954 --> 00:07:27,574
East of the reservoir the Army

206
00:07:27,594 --> 00:07:29,384
regiment known as Task Force

207
00:07:29,404 --> 00:07:31,808
Faith was fighting for its life.

208
00:07:32,199 --> 00:07:33,476
The Ninth Army Group had done

209
00:07:33,496 --> 00:07:35,593
exactly what Mao wanted.

210
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:37,018
It had cut the First Marine

211
00:07:37,038 --> 00:07:39,156
Division into isolated pockets.

212
00:07:39,246 --> 00:07:40,682
Each one surrounded.

213
00:07:40,702 --> 00:07:42,003
Each one alone.

214
00:07:42,416 --> 00:07:44,092
In Tokyo the mood shifted

215
00:07:44,112 --> 00:07:46,505
from confidence to shock.

216
00:07:46,583 --> 00:07:48,543
MacArthur sent word to Washington that

217
00:07:48,563 --> 00:07:52,066
his forces now faced an entirely new war.

218
00:07:52,479 --> 00:07:54,753
On November 29 a relief column of

219
00:07:54,773 --> 00:07:57,813
British Royal Marine commandos and American

220
00:07:57,833 --> 00:08:00,346
troops fought north from Koto-ri.

221
00:08:00,436 --> 00:08:03,408
The Chinese ambushed it in a narrow valley.

222
00:08:03,459 --> 00:08:04,793
Trucks burned.

223
00:08:04,882 --> 00:08:06,705
The column was split apart.

224
00:08:06,815 --> 00:08:08,274
Survivors would call that stretch

225
00:08:08,294 --> 00:08:10,311
of road Hell Fire Valley.

226
00:08:10,381 --> 00:08:12,279
Only part of the force broke through.

227
00:08:12,683 --> 00:08:14,347
For the men at Yudam-ni the

228
00:08:14,367 --> 00:08:16,270
situation was now clear.

229
00:08:16,351 --> 00:08:19,256
They were 14 miles from Hagaru-ri.

230
00:08:19,352 --> 00:08:20,971
Every mile of that road was

231
00:08:20,991 --> 00:08:22,758
controlled by the enemy.

232
00:08:22,778 --> 00:08:23,953
And they had hundreds of

233
00:08:23,973 --> 00:08:26,086
wounded who could not walk.

234
00:08:26,516 --> 00:08:28,089
So what happens to an army when

235
00:08:28,109 --> 00:08:30,036
every road is closed and the

236
00:08:30,056 --> 00:08:32,380
enemy surrounds it on all sides?

237
00:08:32,941 --> 00:08:34,412
Before we answer that question

238
00:08:34,432 --> 00:08:36,927
here is a quick request.

239
00:08:37,034 --> 00:08:39,062
If this story is worth your time

240
00:08:39,082 --> 00:08:41,725
please subscribe to Map of War.

241
00:08:41,803 --> 00:08:43,650
We bring the great battles of history to

242
00:08:43,670 --> 00:08:46,244
life with maps you can actually follow.

243
00:08:46,334 --> 00:08:47,975
Every subscription helps us build

244
00:08:47,995 --> 00:08:49,898
more stories like this one.

245
00:08:50,035 --> 00:08:52,629
Now back to the frozen hills of Chosin.

246
00:08:52,992 --> 00:08:54,036
The answer depended on

247
00:08:54,056 --> 00:08:56,402
one man and one place.

248
00:08:56,433 --> 00:08:58,484
The man was Oliver Smith.

249
00:08:58,505 --> 00:08:59,372
Quiet.

250
00:08:59,393 --> 00:09:00,395
Methodical.

251
00:09:00,415 --> 00:09:03,342
A pipe smoker who rarely raised his voice.

252
00:09:03,431 --> 00:09:04,345
Some of his officers

253
00:09:04,365 --> 00:09:05,858
called him the Professor.

254
00:09:06,258 --> 00:09:08,035
The place was Hagaru-ri.

255
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:09,779
Hagaru-ri sat at the southern

256
00:09:09,799 --> 00:09:11,281
tip of the reservoir.

257
00:09:11,302 --> 00:09:14,183
It was the hub of the entire campaign.

258
00:09:14,352 --> 00:09:15,378
Supplies.

259
00:09:15,399 --> 00:09:16,175
Fuel.

260
00:09:16,196 --> 00:09:17,245
Ammunition.

261
00:09:17,265 --> 00:09:18,645
A field hospital.

262
00:09:18,703 --> 00:09:19,913
And the airstrip Smith had

263
00:09:19,933 --> 00:09:21,474
ordered weeks earlier.

264
00:09:21,857 --> 00:09:24,189
On the night of November 28 it was held

265
00:09:24,209 --> 00:09:26,781
by a single understrength Marine battalion

266
00:09:26,801 --> 00:09:29,303
under Lieutenant Colonel Thomas Ridge.

267
00:09:29,401 --> 00:09:31,867
He had only a few thousand men to defend

268
00:09:31,887 --> 00:09:34,150
a perimeter about 4 miles around.

269
00:09:34,319 --> 00:09:36,460
Truck drivers and engineers and clerks

270
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:39,324
were handed rifles and sent to the line.

271
00:09:39,711 --> 00:09:42,058
The Chinese struck from the southwest and

272
00:09:42,078 --> 00:09:44,675
from a height the Marines called East Hill.

273
00:09:44,746 --> 00:09:45,489
They broke into the

274
00:09:45,509 --> 00:09:47,162
perimeter more than once.

275
00:09:47,330 --> 00:09:49,347
Each time they were pushed back.

276
00:09:49,486 --> 00:09:51,265
Engineers fought through the night and

277
00:09:51,285 --> 00:09:54,280
climbed back onto their bulldozers at dawn.

278
00:09:54,668 --> 00:09:56,610
The airstrip was everything.

279
00:09:56,698 --> 00:09:58,784
Without it the wounded would die and

280
00:09:58,804 --> 00:10:01,060
the division would have no lifeline.

281
00:10:01,224 --> 00:10:02,809
The engineers kept working around

282
00:10:02,829 --> 00:10:04,915
the clock under floodlights even as

283
00:10:04,935 --> 00:10:07,362
enemy fire swept across the field.

284
00:10:07,460 --> 00:10:08,874
The ground was so hard

285
00:10:08,894 --> 00:10:10,535
it fought every blade.

286
00:10:10,659 --> 00:10:11,759
They kept going.

287
00:10:12,163 --> 00:10:14,782
On December 1 the first transport plane

288
00:10:14,802 --> 00:10:17,321
landed on the half-finished strip.

289
00:10:17,418 --> 00:10:20,195
Over the following days aircraft lifted out

290
00:10:20,215 --> 00:10:23,293
more than 4,000 wounded and frostbitten men.

291
00:10:23,416 --> 00:10:24,671
They flew in ammunition

292
00:10:24,691 --> 00:10:26,219
and medical supplies.

293
00:10:26,239 --> 00:10:28,096
They flew in replacements.

294
00:10:28,204 --> 00:10:30,347
For the men surrounded on that plateau

295
00:10:30,367 --> 00:10:33,895
the sound of those engines meant hope.

296
00:10:34,328 --> 00:10:36,278
Meanwhile Smith made the most

297
00:10:36,298 --> 00:10:38,542
important decision of the campaign.

298
00:10:38,606 --> 00:10:40,586
The division would come out together.

299
00:10:40,606 --> 00:10:41,564
All of it.

300
00:10:41,642 --> 00:10:43,225
Every man who could walk.

301
00:10:43,246 --> 00:10:45,797
Every wounded Marine who could not.

302
00:10:45,858 --> 00:10:48,603
As much equipment as the trucks could carry.

303
00:10:48,698 --> 00:10:49,781
Nothing that could help the

304
00:10:49,801 --> 00:10:51,795
enemy would be left behind.

305
00:10:52,486 --> 00:10:54,128
There was talk of flying the Marines

306
00:10:54,148 --> 00:10:56,301
out and abandoning their equipment.

307
00:10:56,425 --> 00:10:57,906
Smith would not hear of it.

308
00:10:57,995 --> 00:11:00,506
They would walk out as a fighting division.

309
00:11:00,919 --> 00:11:02,606
It was during these days at

310
00:11:02,626 --> 00:11:05,668
Hagaru-ri that reporters asked Smith

311
00:11:05,688 --> 00:11:07,625
if the Marines were retreating.

312
00:11:07,679 --> 00:11:09,594
His answer became legend.

313
00:11:09,682 --> 00:11:11,244
The newspapers reported it in

314
00:11:11,264 --> 00:11:13,622
four famous words and a promise.

315
00:11:13,698 --> 00:11:15,719
Retreat, hell! We're just

316
00:11:15,739 --> 00:11:17,503
attacking in another direction.

317
00:11:17,889 --> 00:11:21,249
Historians still debate his exact words.

318
00:11:21,384 --> 00:11:23,811
Smith later explained what he meant.

319
00:11:23,874 --> 00:11:26,574
When you are surrounded there is no rear.

320
00:11:26,662 --> 00:11:28,429
There is nowhere to retreat to.

321
00:11:28,449 --> 00:11:29,942
You can only break out.

322
00:11:30,001 --> 00:11:32,864
But the meaning was the same either way.

323
00:11:33,249 --> 00:11:35,219
The Marines would not run.

324
00:11:35,298 --> 00:11:37,144
They would fight their way out.

325
00:11:37,272 --> 00:11:38,287
First the regiments at

326
00:11:38,307 --> 00:11:40,153
Yudam-ni had to reach him.

327
00:11:40,549 --> 00:11:43,636
On December 1 they began their breakout.

328
00:11:43,724 --> 00:11:45,308
14 miles.

329
00:11:45,436 --> 00:11:47,134
Wounded men were strapped to trucks

330
00:11:47,154 --> 00:11:49,160
and tanks and the hoods of jeeps.

331
00:11:49,215 --> 00:11:51,222
Some of the dead were carried too.

332
00:11:51,242 --> 00:11:52,316
And between them and

333
00:11:52,336 --> 00:11:55,109
Hagaru-ri sat Toktong Pass.

334
00:11:55,495 --> 00:11:58,158
On a barren hill above Toktong Pass a single

335
00:11:58,178 --> 00:12:00,827
company had been holding out for 5 days.

336
00:12:01,599 --> 00:12:02,774
Fox Company of the Second

337
00:12:02,794 --> 00:12:04,708
Battalion Seventh Marines.

338
00:12:04,729 --> 00:12:08,350
Around 240 men under Captain William Barber.

339
00:12:08,424 --> 00:12:10,543
Their job was to keep the pass open.

340
00:12:10,651 --> 00:12:11,815
On the very first night the

341
00:12:11,835 --> 00:12:13,430
Chinese surrounded them.

342
00:12:13,816 --> 00:12:15,989
The attacks came night after night.

343
00:12:16,063 --> 00:12:17,637
The Marines held a small ring

344
00:12:17,657 --> 00:12:19,650
of foxholes on the slope.

345
00:12:19,773 --> 00:12:22,200
Planes dropped ammunition and food.

346
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:24,623
Some bundles landed among the enemy.

347
00:12:24,747 --> 00:12:26,275
Men crawled out under fire

348
00:12:26,295 --> 00:12:27,907
to drag back the rest.

349
00:12:28,358 --> 00:12:29,352
Barber was shot in the

350
00:12:29,372 --> 00:12:30,785
leg on the second day.

351
00:12:30,805 --> 00:12:32,528
He refused to be relieved.

352
00:12:32,548 --> 00:12:34,622
He was carried on a stretcher from position

353
00:12:34,642 --> 00:12:36,715
to position to direct the defense.

354
00:12:36,815 --> 00:12:38,912
By the end most of the company had been

355
00:12:38,932 --> 00:12:41,928
killed or wounded or crippled by frostbite.

356
00:12:41,965 --> 00:12:43,674
But Fox Hill never fell.

357
00:12:44,071 --> 00:12:45,843
The men at Yudam-ni knew that if the

358
00:12:45,863 --> 00:12:48,749
pass fell they would never make it out.

359
00:12:48,856 --> 00:12:50,179
So Lieutenant Colonel Raymond

360
00:12:50,199 --> 00:12:52,625
Davis was given a desperate task.

361
00:12:52,745 --> 00:12:54,216
He would take the First Battalion

362
00:12:54,236 --> 00:12:57,255
Seventh Marines off the road entirely.

363
00:12:57,423 --> 00:12:58,758
They would march cross-country

364
00:12:58,778 --> 00:13:00,386
at night over the mountains and

365
00:13:00,406 --> 00:13:02,760
reach Fox Company from behind.

366
00:13:03,149 --> 00:13:05,376
On the night of December 1 Davis

367
00:13:05,396 --> 00:13:07,442
led his men into the dark.

368
00:13:07,587 --> 00:13:09,706
Snow lay knee-deep and deeper.

369
00:13:09,784 --> 00:13:12,336
The temperature sank far below zero.

370
00:13:12,425 --> 00:13:13,747
There was no trail.

371
00:13:13,916 --> 00:13:15,819
Some men dozed off on their feet

372
00:13:15,839 --> 00:13:17,663
and had to be shaken awake.

373
00:13:17,786 --> 00:13:20,110
The battalion steered by the stars and

374
00:13:20,130 --> 00:13:22,841
fought small battles along the ridgelines.

375
00:13:23,236 --> 00:13:24,922
On the morning of December

376
00:13:24,942 --> 00:13:26,733
2 they reached Fox Hill.

377
00:13:26,830 --> 00:13:28,336
The survivors of Fox Company

378
00:13:28,356 --> 00:13:29,701
came out to meet them.

379
00:13:29,799 --> 00:13:32,136
Both Barber and Davis would later

380
00:13:32,156 --> 00:13:33,762
receive the Medal of Honor.

381
00:13:34,244 --> 00:13:35,726
Down on the road the main

382
00:13:35,746 --> 00:13:37,933
column ground forward.

383
00:13:38,102 --> 00:13:39,676
Infantry cleared the ridges on

384
00:13:39,696 --> 00:13:42,032
both sides while trucks and artillery

385
00:13:42,052 --> 00:13:43,841
crawled along between them.

386
00:13:44,010 --> 00:13:45,685
Every hill had to be taken.

387
00:13:45,774 --> 00:13:48,189
Every roadblock had to be broken.

388
00:13:48,357 --> 00:13:50,704
Marine Corsair fighters flew so close

389
00:13:50,724 --> 00:13:52,834
overhead that spent shell casings

390
00:13:52,854 --> 00:13:55,192
rained down on the troops below.

391
00:13:55,598 --> 00:13:57,495
It took the column nearly 80

392
00:13:57,515 --> 00:13:59,789
hours to cover those 14 miles.

393
00:13:59,889 --> 00:14:02,564
On the evening of December 3 the lead

394
00:14:02,584 --> 00:14:05,501
elements marched down into Hagaru-ri.

395
00:14:05,598 --> 00:14:07,581
Behind them came the wounded and

396
00:14:07,601 --> 00:14:09,766
the frostbitten and the guns.

397
00:14:09,882 --> 00:14:11,307
Witnesses remembered that some

398
00:14:11,327 --> 00:14:12,423
of the men were singing the

399
00:14:12,443 --> 00:14:14,677
Marines' Hymn as they came in.

400
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:17,034
The division was together again.

401
00:14:17,103 --> 00:14:18,209
Almost.

402
00:14:18,268 --> 00:14:20,945
From the east the news was grim.

403
00:14:21,021 --> 00:14:23,971
Task Force Faith had been destroyed.

404
00:14:24,081 --> 00:14:27,099
Of roughly 3,000 Army soldiers only

405
00:14:27,119 --> 00:14:30,742
about 1,000 reached Marine lines alive.

406
00:14:30,908 --> 00:14:32,471
Many of them had crawled across the

407
00:14:32,491 --> 00:14:34,533
frozen surface of the reservoir.

408
00:14:34,916 --> 00:14:37,748
But Hagaru-ri was only a stopping point.

409
00:14:37,834 --> 00:14:40,978
Koto-ri lay 11 more miles to the south.

410
00:14:41,049 --> 00:14:43,282
And the Chinese were waiting there too.

411
00:14:43,675 --> 00:14:45,454
On December 6 the division

412
00:14:45,474 --> 00:14:47,285
broke out of Hagaru-ri.

413
00:14:47,364 --> 00:14:50,017
It took nearly 2 days of hard fighting

414
00:14:50,037 --> 00:14:52,579
to cover the 11 miles to Koto-ri.

415
00:14:52,634 --> 00:14:54,246
10,000 men in a column

416
00:14:54,266 --> 00:14:56,345
that stretched for miles.

417
00:14:56,749 --> 00:14:59,119
At Koto-ri the legendary Colonel

418
00:14:59,139 --> 00:15:00,815
Lewis Chesty Puller held the

419
00:15:00,835 --> 00:15:02,716
town with the First Marines.

420
00:15:02,736 --> 00:15:04,139
He is credited with a famous

421
00:15:04,159 --> 00:15:06,210
line about being surrounded.

422
00:15:06,333 --> 00:15:07,952
They are on our left.

423
00:15:07,973 --> 00:15:09,477
They are on our right.

424
00:15:09,497 --> 00:15:10,945
They are in front of us.

425
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:12,448
They are behind us.

426
00:15:12,537 --> 00:15:14,406
They can't get away this time.

427
00:15:14,818 --> 00:15:17,307
But at Koto-ri the Marines faced a problem

428
00:15:17,327 --> 00:15:19,628
that no amount of courage could solve.

429
00:15:19,775 --> 00:15:21,099
South of the town the road

430
00:15:21,119 --> 00:15:23,204
dropped into Funchilin Pass.

431
00:15:23,224 --> 00:15:25,742
A steep descent toward the coastal plain.

432
00:15:26,142 --> 00:15:27,783
At one point the road crossed

433
00:15:27,803 --> 00:15:29,866
a narrow gap above the pipes of

434
00:15:29,886 --> 00:15:31,983
a hydroelectric power plant.

435
00:15:32,059 --> 00:15:34,657
The Chinese had blown the bridge there.

436
00:15:34,686 --> 00:15:37,158
The gap was about 29 feet wide.

437
00:15:37,247 --> 00:15:39,344
On one side rose a cliff.

438
00:15:39,459 --> 00:15:41,653
On the other side was a sheer drop.

439
00:15:42,035 --> 00:15:43,644
Without that bridge no truck and

440
00:15:43,664 --> 00:15:46,237
no tank and no gun could pass.

441
00:15:46,375 --> 00:15:49,442
14,000 men and well over 1,000 vehicles

442
00:15:49,462 --> 00:15:52,223
would be trapped on the plateau.

443
00:15:52,611 --> 00:15:54,057
The division engineer Lieutenant

444
00:15:54,077 --> 00:15:56,674
Colonel John Partridge had an answer.

445
00:15:56,772 --> 00:15:59,358
No one had ever tried it in combat before.

446
00:15:59,436 --> 00:16:01,996
They would drop a bridge from the sky.

447
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:04,979
In Japan Air Force crews loaded

448
00:16:04,999 --> 00:16:07,243
steel bridge sections onto

449
00:16:07,263 --> 00:16:10,096
C-119 Flying Boxcar transports.

450
00:16:10,185 --> 00:16:12,954
Each section weighed about 2.5 tons.

451
00:16:13,008 --> 00:16:14,456
A test drop smashed one

452
00:16:14,476 --> 00:16:16,050
section into the ground.

453
00:16:16,219 --> 00:16:18,377
So they switched to larger parachutes.

454
00:16:18,771 --> 00:16:20,556
On December 7 8 sections

455
00:16:20,576 --> 00:16:22,757
floated down over Koto-ri.

456
00:16:22,874 --> 00:16:24,822
One was damaged on landing.

457
00:16:24,946 --> 00:16:27,281
One drifted into Chinese lines.

458
00:16:27,405 --> 00:16:29,718
The rest landed where they were needed.

459
00:16:29,807 --> 00:16:30,830
It was enough.

460
00:16:31,213 --> 00:16:32,568
But first the Marines had to

461
00:16:32,588 --> 00:16:34,570
take the heights above the pass.

462
00:16:34,648 --> 00:16:37,470
On the night of December 8 a battalion of

463
00:16:37,490 --> 00:16:40,243
the First Marines climbed toward a peak

464
00:16:40,263 --> 00:16:43,255
called Hill 1081 in a blinding blizzard.

465
00:16:43,361 --> 00:16:46,083
They attacked Chinese positions in the snow.

466
00:16:46,104 --> 00:16:48,587
The fighting lasted into the next day.

467
00:16:48,609 --> 00:16:50,102
When it ended the high ground

468
00:16:50,122 --> 00:16:51,941
belonged to the Marines.

469
00:16:52,344 --> 00:16:54,662
On December 9 the engineers hauled the

470
00:16:54,682 --> 00:16:57,355
bridge sections down the road on trucks.

471
00:16:57,453 --> 00:16:59,754
They laid the steel across the gap.

472
00:16:59,814 --> 00:17:01,865
The span was barely wide enough.

473
00:17:01,930 --> 00:17:04,629
Tanks could ride on the two steel treadways.

474
00:17:04,684 --> 00:17:05,643
Jeeps needed wooden

475
00:17:05,663 --> 00:17:07,566
panels laid between them.

476
00:17:07,621 --> 00:17:09,558
One wrong move and a vehicle would

477
00:17:09,578 --> 00:17:11,576
tumble into the gorge below.

478
00:17:11,957 --> 00:17:14,429
That night the column began to cross.

479
00:17:14,450 --> 00:17:15,375
Trucks.

480
00:17:15,395 --> 00:17:16,489
Artillery.

481
00:17:16,509 --> 00:17:17,684
Ambulances.

482
00:17:17,705 --> 00:17:19,096
Tanks at the rear.

483
00:17:19,116 --> 00:17:21,498
Thousands of Korean refugees followed

484
00:17:21,518 --> 00:17:23,458
behind the Marines in the snow.

485
00:17:23,861 --> 00:17:25,582
The impossible had happened.

486
00:17:25,702 --> 00:17:27,890
A bridge had fallen from the sky and an

487
00:17:27,910 --> 00:17:30,195
entire division had walked across it.

488
00:17:30,590 --> 00:17:31,903
Below the pass the road

489
00:17:31,923 --> 00:17:33,723
opened toward the sea.

490
00:17:33,891 --> 00:17:35,908
Army troops waited at the bottom.

491
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:37,732
By December 11 the last Marines

492
00:17:37,752 --> 00:17:39,823
had come down from the mountains.

493
00:17:40,215 --> 00:17:42,879
The Marines reached Hungnam on the coast.

494
00:17:42,968 --> 00:17:45,091
But the story did not end there.

495
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:47,539
In mid-December the United Nations

496
00:17:47,559 --> 00:17:48,882
carried out one of the largest

497
00:17:48,902 --> 00:17:52,035
evacuations by sea in military history.

498
00:17:52,143 --> 00:17:54,753
Over about 2 weeks ships carried more

499
00:17:54,773 --> 00:17:57,483
than 100,000 troops out of Hungnam.

500
00:17:57,593 --> 00:17:59,605
They also carried nearly 100,000

501
00:17:59,625 --> 00:18:02,061
Korean civilians fleeing the North.

502
00:18:02,493 --> 00:18:05,011
One freighter called the Meredith Victory

503
00:18:05,031 --> 00:18:07,595
was built to carry just 12 passengers.

504
00:18:07,675 --> 00:18:10,887
It took on about 14,000 refugees.

505
00:18:10,908 --> 00:18:13,516
Not one life was lost on the voyage.

506
00:18:13,685 --> 00:18:16,211
5 babies were born along the way.

507
00:18:16,614 --> 00:18:18,063
On Christmas Eve demolition

508
00:18:18,083 --> 00:18:19,644
teams blew up the port.

509
00:18:19,699 --> 00:18:21,847
The last ships pulled away.

510
00:18:22,263 --> 00:18:23,994
The cost had been terrible.

511
00:18:24,055 --> 00:18:26,239
The First Marine Division suffered

512
00:18:26,259 --> 00:18:28,678
more than 4,000 battle casualties.

513
00:18:28,802 --> 00:18:30,011
Thousands more were

514
00:18:30,031 --> 00:18:32,105
struck down by frostbite.

515
00:18:32,152 --> 00:18:34,878
Across all United Nations forces the

516
00:18:34,898 --> 00:18:38,175
campaign cost roughly 17,000 casualties.

517
00:18:38,564 --> 00:18:41,169
But the cost to the enemy was far greater.

518
00:18:41,237 --> 00:18:42,890
Chinese records later showed the

519
00:18:42,910 --> 00:18:44,904
Ninth Army Group lost tens of

520
00:18:44,924 --> 00:18:47,158
thousands killed and wounded.

521
00:18:47,238 --> 00:18:48,971
Tens of thousands more were

522
00:18:48,991 --> 00:18:51,099
taken out of action by the cold.

523
00:18:51,267 --> 00:18:53,285
The army group sent to annihilate the

524
00:18:53,305 --> 00:18:56,149
Marines was so badly broken that it

525
00:18:56,169 --> 00:18:58,616
could not return to battle for months.

526
00:18:59,025 --> 00:19:01,565
That was the strategic meaning of Chosin.

527
00:19:01,712 --> 00:19:03,377
Mao had ordered the destruction

528
00:19:03,397 --> 00:19:05,323
of the First Marine Division.

529
00:19:05,417 --> 00:19:07,161
Instead the Marines fought their way

530
00:19:07,181 --> 00:19:09,778
through a force many times their size.

531
00:19:09,798 --> 00:19:11,440
They brought out their wounded.

532
00:19:11,460 --> 00:19:13,124
They brought out their guns.

533
00:19:13,222 --> 00:19:15,115
And they left one of China's finest

534
00:19:15,135 --> 00:19:17,732
army groups too shattered to pursue.

535
00:19:18,129 --> 00:19:19,668
The men who survived called

536
00:19:19,688 --> 00:19:22,387
themselves the Chosin Few.

537
00:19:22,457 --> 00:19:24,155
For the rest of their lives many

538
00:19:24,175 --> 00:19:26,340
carried the scars of that winter.

539
00:19:26,361 --> 00:19:27,649
Numb fingers.

540
00:19:27,671 --> 00:19:28,970
Damaged feet.

541
00:19:29,059 --> 00:19:31,012
Memories that never thawed.

542
00:19:31,406 --> 00:19:33,666
They also carried something else.

543
00:19:33,721 --> 00:19:35,762
The knowledge that they had walked out of

544
00:19:35,782 --> 00:19:38,516
a trap that was meant to be their grave.

545
00:19:38,593 --> 00:19:40,383
Historians have compared the march

546
00:19:40,403 --> 00:19:42,056
to the retreat of Xenophon's

547
00:19:42,076 --> 00:19:44,946
Ten Thousand in ancient Greece.

548
00:19:45,026 --> 00:19:47,568
A fighting withdrawal so disciplined that

549
00:19:47,588 --> 00:19:50,095
it became a kind of victory of its own.

550
00:19:50,560 --> 00:19:52,076
17 days.

551
00:19:52,244 --> 00:19:53,760
78 miles.

552
00:19:53,930 --> 00:19:55,082
40 below.

553
00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:58,104
And one simple decision made in the snow.

554
00:19:58,125 --> 00:19:59,618
We are not retreating.

555
00:19:59,673 --> 00:20:01,140
We are coming out together.

556
